# Literature and claim audit (accessed 2026-10-20)

There is no verified, universally best IBP heuristic across all topologies, target
sets, basis choices, and cost models. The frontier is a set of conditional results.
This pilot establishes infrastructure and a local comparison, not a new state of the art.

| Primary source | What is established/reported | Relevance and limitation |
|---|---|---|
| [Laporta, hep-ph/0102033](https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0102033) | Systematic reduction and master-integral algorithms using IBP systems | Source of the elimination baseline; our small implementation is not the original program |
| [von Hippel & Wilhelm, 2502.05121v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.05121v1) | FunSearch and typed genetic programming rediscover useful seeding rules | Read in detail; see below |
| [Song et al., 2502.09544v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.09544v1) | FunSearch-derived priorities tested on several integral settings; author-reported large seed-count reductions | Seed count, runtime, and memory must be separated; their reported factor 3058 is conditional, not a universal runtime gain |
| [Lange, Usovitsch & Wu, Kira 3, 2505.20197v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.20197v1) | Improved seeding and equation selection; large gains over Kira 2.3 on selected multi-loop cases | A serious future baseline must use Kira 3, not only the older rectangular strategy |
| [Smirnov & Zeng, FIRE 7, 2510.07150v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07150v2) | Modular reconstruction, rational-polynomial improvements, and symbolic presolve | Production comparator; not installed or timed here |
| [Lee, LiteRed 1.4, 1310.1145v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.1145v1) | Mathematica package for multiloop reduction | Symbolic rules and symmetry analysis are relevant; package paper identified, not locally reproduced |
| [von Manteuffel & Studerus, Reduze 2, 1201.4330](https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.4330) | Distributed Laporta variant and topology equivalence detection | Scheduling and equivalent integral reuse are established directions |
| [Peraro, FiniteFlow, 1905.08019](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.08019) | Finite-field functional reconstruction with dataflow graphs | Avoiding expression swell and sharing calculations are established; sampled values alone are not our certificate |
| [Wu et al., NeatIBP 1.1 + Kira, 2502.20778v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.20778v1) | Syzygy-system reduction, Kira interface, spanning cuts, module simplification | A different route to compact systems, outside this one-loop engine |
| [Berman et al., tube seeding, 2606.10698v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.10698v1) | Sparse paths between targets and masters; linear seed growth with numerator rank in described construction | More recent frontier: demonstrated high-rank nonplanar two-loop reductions over a finite field; does not establish our symbolic identities |
| [Shih, SAILIR, 2604.05034v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.05034v1) | Self-supervised classifier with beam search on triangle-box reductions | Author reports a memory/runtime tradeoff; training and parallel-worker budgets must be counted |
| [Bitoun et al., 1712.09215](https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.09215) | Parametric annihilators and master counts; explicit massless propagator example | Source for the independently reproduced analytic bubble ratio |

## The requested paper, with scope intact

Section 2 compares rectangular seeds, a dot cap (the golden rule), and a
sector-dependent numerator cap. For its triangle-box setup the listed seed counts
are 14,588, 2,148, and 92. These choices differ in bounds, so they are not an isolated
ablation of one feature. The later 88-seed rule omits additional three-line seeds.
The authors explicitly leave open whether that last gain is peculiar to the example.
Their fitness rewards successful master-basis reduction with fewer seeds; it is a
proxy for computational cost, rather than a direct runtime objective. They use Kira
for the reduction test.

The direct binary subset genetic search was slow in their reported experiment.
Typed genetic programming benefited from the structure found during FunSearch.
This motivates a constrained policy grammar, strong completeness checks, accounting
for failed trials, and evaluation on new families. The paper's results do not prove
that a donated 30–60 minute session can reproduce its entire search. We have not
reproduced the triangle-box 88-seed result in this pilot.

## Current frontier and settled questions

Kira 3 and FIRE 7 are newer production references than the requested February 2025
paper. The June 2026 tube-seeding work is especially relevant to donated searches
for seed paths, and provides [public proof-of-principle code](https://github.com/andreslunagodoy/tube_seeding).
No source-backed claim here says that our winner beats any of these implementations.

A few numerical checks cannot establish a rational identity: a nonzero polynomial
can vanish on every tested point. A smaller seed set is not useful if targets remain
unreduced, or if it changes the designated basis to extra apparent masters. A
single-instance improvement does not settle cross-family generalization. These are
acceptance requirements, not alleged impossibility results for heuristic search.
Random subset search was slow in the requested paper's setting; that is not proof
that every subset search is ineffective. Kira's discussion of random equation
reordering likewise has an implementation- and case-dependent scope.

## Audit of the user's premises

- IBP reduction is an important computational bottleneck: supported by the solver
  and AI papers above; severity depends on the calculation.
- More efficient selection or elimination can improve it: supported by published
  algorithms and authors' case studies; general superiority remains an open question.
- Ordinary CPUs can complete useful small cases: now locally tested by this pilot;
  large multi-loop/high-rank cases are not implied.
- AI-assisted heuristic discovery has precedent: confirmed by the requested paper,
  Song et al., and newer tube-seeding/SAILIR work. Novelty of any local winner is
  unverified until compared with these methods and reviewed by a domain expert.

Only primary papers and their identified software repositories inform this audit.
Web search snippets and third-party AI reviews are not treated as evidence.


